1
00:00:00,502 --> 00:00:05,388
Follow a received filing through court review, a decision, and a retained record.

2
00:00:07,468 --> 00:00:10,691
The clerk starts in the review queue for an assigned court.

3
00:00:11,361 --> 00:00:14,984
Search by case title, case number, or filing number.

4
00:00:15,666 --> 00:00:19,869
Status and elapsed-time filters help staff find the work they need.

5
00:00:21,935 --> 00:00:25,763
Open the Harbor Services filing submitted in the attorney walkthrough.

6
00:00:26,434 --> 00:00:31,706
The review shows the received case information and submission number, alongside the queue.

7
00:00:33,802 --> 00:00:38,455
Inspect the complaint and summons, and download the filed originals for review.

8
00:00:39,125 --> 00:00:43,973
Document labels and confidentiality indicators stay with each attachment.

9
00:00:46,068 --> 00:00:50,902
The receipt separates the submission time, fee record, and attached documents.

10
00:00:51,585 --> 00:00:55,014
Here, the payment is explicitly marked as simulated.

11
00:00:55,683 --> 00:00:59,964
Court staff can inspect the same retained record that the filer sees.

12
00:01:02,035 --> 00:01:07,242
To demonstrate a correction request, return this second sample with a specific reason.

13
00:01:07,913 --> 00:01:11,445
Staff ask the filer to clarify the requested relief.

14
00:01:12,114 --> 00:01:16,678
Return for Correction allows another submission while keeping the earlier record.

15
00:01:18,735 --> 00:01:22,318
For the completed Harbor Services filing, choose Accept.

16
00:01:22,989 --> 00:01:26,791
This records the court's decision and opens a case in the demo.

17
00:01:27,461 --> 00:01:30,762
The filing leaves the pending queue after acceptance.

18
00:01:32,835 --> 00:01:39,021
The history now contains a separate acceptance entry, with the reviewer, time, and court case number.

19
00:01:39,705 --> 00:01:46,447
The original submission remains below it. Receipt and decision are distinct, traceable events.

20
00:01:48,502 --> 00:01:52,008
Open Court Cases to find the new case and its docket.

21
00:01:52,691 --> 00:01:57,241
Case access and document publication are separate court-controlled actions.

22
00:01:57,923 --> 00:02:02,642
Accepting a filing does not automatically publish every uploaded document.

23
00:02:04,735 --> 00:02:09,079
The court has recorded its decision, and both sides retain the history.

24
00:02:09,749 --> 00:02:12,624
This demonstration uses fictional records.

25
00:02:13,293 --> 00:02:17,883
Live payment and court-system connections remain implementation choices.
